# The drive as an ELECTRICAL machine, for the current inner loop.
#
# `NonidealMotor` (dyad/nonideal_motor.dyad) is a torque source with a fitted
# scale and a dead band: the command IS the torque, and the only thing between
# them is arithmetic. That model cannot carry a current loop, because it has no
# current. `DCPlanarMotor` puts the machine back: the H bridge applies
# `duty * Vbus` across an R-L winding, the back EMF takes `Ke * w` away, and
# the winding current makes the torque. The measured current comes out on a
# port, so a controller can close a loop around it.
#
# WHAT THE BENCH OF 2026-09-22 MEASURED (BalansBotDeploy/calibration/data/drive_calibration_*.csv,
# wheels free, fitted by BalansBotDeploy/calibration/fit_current.jl). Every number is a RATIO,
# because the bus voltage was not measured:
#
#   quantity                           motor A         motor B
#   stall current per duty  Vbus/R      2.456 A         2.107 A
#   back EMF                Ke/Vbus     0.03721 /V      0.03771 /V
#   Coulomb friction        tau_c/Kt    0.0650 A        0.0623 A
#   viscous friction        d/Kt        0.00231 A s/rad 0.00267 A s/rad
#   break-away friction     tau_s/Kt    0.1915 A        0.1203 A
#   electrical constant     L/R         5.56 ms         5.40 ms
#   mechanical constant     Iw/d_total  47.5 ms         56.7 ms
#
# so with a bus voltage `Vbus`:
#
#   Kt = Ke = Vbus * (Ke/Vbus)    R = Vbus / (stall per duty)    L = (L/R) * R
#   tau_c = (tau_c/Kt) * Kt       d = (d/Kt) * Kt                tau_s = (tau_s/Kt) * Kt
#   Iw    = (Iw/d_total) * (Kt * Ke / R + d)
#
# Ke is NOT the slope of the steady-speed line: that slope leaves the `i R` drop
# in, which is 5 % of the bus at full duty and goes straight into Kt and Iw. It
# comes from `Ke = (|duty| Vbus - i R) / |w|` at every level above |duty| = 0.3,
# with R from the stalled part of the ramp-up. CHECKED against a meter: the DC
# no-load current at duty 1.0 comes out 0.1239 A against the measured 0.1215 A
# for motor A and 0.1287 against 0.1274 for motor B, and nothing was fitted to it.
#
# VBUS WAS MEASURED ON 2026-09-22: 7.68 V with a meter across the motor supply
# during a full-duty hold of both motors, wheels free, on a 2S lithium pack at
# mid charge. Every default below is that 7.68 V. A 2S pack swings 6.4 to 8.4 V
# over a discharge and `Kt`, `R`, `L` and `Iw` are all PROPORTIONAL to it, so
# refit or re-measure when it matters; a current loop tuned by the same formulas
# keeps its bandwidth, because `K_i = 2 pi f L / Vbus` moves with `L` and `Vbus`
# together.
#
# The old components stay exactly as they were, so every analysis that runs on
# `NonidealMotor` and `NonidealPlanarDyadBot` keeps running.

"""
The velocity-dependent half of `NonidealTorque`, as a block of its own.

`tau = -(tau_c + (tau_s - tau_c) * exp(-(w / w_s)^2)) * tanh(w / w_eps)`

The friction is `tau_s` at rest and falls to `tau_c` once the shaft slides, and
the sign follows the shaft. `NonidealTorque` carries the same expression with a
dead band and a torque-constant error in front of it; those two belong to a
drive that is commanded in torque, and `DCPlanarMotor` has neither, because the
dead band IS the friction once the winding is modelled.

`tanh(w / w_eps)` means the shaft never quite sticks, it creeps very slowly, so
`w_eps` must be small for a hold to look right. True hysteresis (break-away that
depends on the history of approach, presliding displacement) still needs an
internal state, as in LuGre.
"""
component StribeckFriction
  "Shaft speed, rad/s"
  w = RealInput() {^w}
  "Friction torque acting on the shaft, N*m"
  tau = RealOutput() {^tau}
  "Coulomb friction torque in motion, N*m"
  parameter tau_c::Real = 0.0193
  "Static friction torque at rest, N*m. Equal to tau_c disables the Stribeck term."
  parameter tau_s::Real = 0.0552
  "Stribeck speed: how fast the friction falls from tau_s to tau_c, rad/s"
  parameter w_s::Real = 0.1
  "Speed scale of the friction smoothing, rad/s"
  parameter w_eps::Real = 0.5
relations
  tau = -(tau_c + (tau_s - tau_c) * exp(-(w / w_s) ^ 2)) * tanh(w / w_eps)
metadata {
  "Dyad": {"icons": {"default": "dyad://BlockComponents/Gain.svg"}},
  "_links": {
    "w": {
      "Dyad": {
        "placement": {
          "diagram": {"iconName": "default", "x1": 60, "y1": 460, "x2": 140, "y2": 540, "rot": 0}
        },
        "tags": []
      }
    },
    "tau": {
      "Dyad": {
        "placement": {
          "diagram": {"iconName": "default", "x1": 860, "y1": 460, "x2": 940, "y2": 540, "rot": 0}
        },
        "tags": []
      }
    }
  }
}
end

"""
Planar motor driven by a PWM duty through a real DC machine.

The sibling of `NonidealMotor`: the same `PartialTwoFrames` mechanics, the same
revolute, damper, fixed support and speed sensor, and the same friction law.
What changes is everything in front of the shaft. Instead of a torque source
fed by an algebraic drive model there is a circuit:

    duty --> Gain(Vbus) --> VoltageSource --- R --- L --- ammeter --- EMF(Kt)
                                  |                                    |
                                  +-------------- ground --------------+

with the EMF rotor on the shaft. So

    Vbus * duty = R * i + L * di/dt + Kt * w
    tau_shaft   = Kt * i - d * w - friction(w)

The H bridge is a DRV8411A and the runtime's PWM pattern alternates drive and
brake, never coast except at exactly duty 0, so the average applied voltage IS
`duty * Vbus` and no extra factor belongs here.

N_MOTORS IN ONE MACHINE. The robot has 2 motors on one duty command and, in the
planar model, on one wheel. `n_motors` lumps them exactly: `n` identical motors
in parallel across the same bridge and rigid on the same shaft are one machine
with `R/n`, `L/n`, the same `Kt = Ke`, and `n` times the friction. The circuit
above then carries the TOTAL current, and `current` is the total divided by `n`,
which is the MEAN per-motor current -- what the robot measures, since the runtime
averages the 2 ADC channels. So `R`, `L`, `Kt`, `d`, `tau_c` and `tau_s` are all
PER-MOTOR values straight out of the bench, and the wheel torque per ampere of
`current` is `n_motors * Kt`, not `Kt`. A current loop must use the former.

`current` is the SIGNED winding current in amperes, positive when the duty is
positive. The robot's sensor is not signed: the DRV8411A IPROPI pin mirrors the
low-side FET current, so the ADC reads a magnitude and reads 0 in coast. The
runtime puts the sign back from the last applied duty; see
`BalansRuntime/src/dyad_controller.jl`.

Parameter defaults are the 2026-09-22 bench at the measured bus voltage of
7.68 V. See the header of this file for the ratios they come from.
"""
component DCPlanarMotor
  extends MultibodyComponents.PlanarMechanics.PartialTwoFrames
  "PWM duty of the H bridge, -1 to 1"
  duty = RealInput() {^duty}
  "Winding current, signed, A"
  current = RealOutput() {^current}
  "Shaft speed relative to the housing, rad/s"
  w = RealOutput() {^w}
  bus = BlockComponents.Math.Gain(k = Vbus) {^bus}
  source = ElectricalComponents.Analog.Sources.VoltageSource() {^source}
  ground = ElectricalComponents.Analog.Basic.Ground() {^ground}
  resistor = ElectricalComponents.Analog.Basic.Resistor(R = R / n_motors) {^resistor}
  inductor = ElectricalComponents.Analog.Basic.Inductor(L = L / n_motors) {^inductor}
  ammeter = ElectricalComponents.Analog.Sensors.CurrentSensor() {^ammeter}
  mean_current = BlockComponents.Math.Gain(k = 1 / n_motors) {^mean_current}
  emf = ElectricalComponents.Analog.Basic.RotationalEMF(k = Kt) {^emf}
  motor_rotation = MultibodyComponents.PlanarMechanics.Revolute(render = false) {^motor_rotation}
  damper = RotationalComponents.Components.Damper(d = n_motors * d) {^damper}
  fixed = RotationalComponents.Components.Fixed() {^fixed}
  speed = RotationalComponents.Sensors.VelocitySensor() {^speed}
  friction = StribeckFriction(tau_c = n_motors * tau_c, tau_s = n_motors * tau_s, w_s = w_s, w_eps = w_eps) {^friction}
  frictionsource = RotationalComponents.Sources.TorqueSource() {^frictionsource}
  "How many identical motors share this command and this shaft. 2 on the robot."
  structural parameter n_motors::Real = 2
  "Bus voltage across the H bridge, V. Measured 7.68 V at full duty on 2026-09-22; a 2S pack swings 6.4 to 8.4 V over a discharge."
  parameter Vbus::Real = 7.68
  "Winding resistance of ONE motor, Ohm. Vbus / 2.28 from the stall current per duty."
  parameter R::Real = 3.3864171
  "Winding inductance of ONE motor, H. 0.00548 * R from the electrical time constant of the chirp."
  parameter L::Real = 0.0185392
  "Torque and back-EMF constant of ONE motor at the wheel, N*m/A. Vbus * Ke/Vbus, with the i R drop taken out level by level."
  parameter Kt::Real = 0.2877012
  "Viscous friction of ONE motor, N*m*s/rad. 0.00249 * Kt from the steady-speed line."
  parameter d::Real = 7.1601e-4
  "Coulomb friction torque of ONE motor in motion, N*m. 0.0637 * Kt from the steady-speed intercept."
  parameter tau_c::Real = 0.0183125
  "Static friction torque of ONE motor at rest, N*m. The break-away current times Kt."
  parameter tau_s::Real = 0.0447701
  "Stribeck speed, rad/s. Not measured; the shape between rest and sliding."
  parameter w_s::Real = 0.1
  "Speed scale of the friction smoothing, rad/s"
  parameter w_eps::Real = 0.5
relations
  # The winding starts empty. Without it the initialization of any model that
  # holds this motor is one equation short: the inductor current is a state and
  # nothing else fixes it.
  initial inductor.i = 0.0
  connect(duty, bus.u) {^id1}
  connect(bus.y, source.V) {^id2}
  connect(source.p, resistor.p) {^id3}
  connect(resistor.n, inductor.p) {^id4}
  connect(inductor.n, ammeter.p) {^id5}
  connect(ammeter.n, emf.p) {^id6}
  connect(emf.n, source.n, ground.g) {^id7}
  connect(ammeter.i, mean_current.u) {^id8}
  connect(mean_current.y, current) {^id8b}
  connect(emf.rotor, motor_rotation.flange_a, damper.spline_a, speed.spline, frictionsource.spline) {^id9}
  connect(emf.housing, damper.spline_b, fixed.spline, frictionsource.support) {^id10}
  connect(speed.w, friction.w, w) {^id11}
  connect(friction.tau, frictionsource.tau) {^id12}
  connect(frame_a, motor_rotation.frame_a) {^id13}
  connect(motor_rotation.frame_b, frame_b) {^id14}
metadata {
  "Dyad": {"icons": {"default": "dyad://MultibodyComponents/Revolute.svg"}}
}
end

"""
`NonidealPlanarDyadBot` with the `DCPlanarMotor`: the robot a current loop can
be closed around.

The mechanics are identical, parameter for parameter, so a run of this model and
a run of `NonidealPlanarDyadBot` differ only in the drive. The interface differs
in two places: the input is a `duty` between -1 and 1 instead of a torque in
N*m, and there is one more output, `current`, the signed winding current that
`BalansBotDeploy.CurrentSensor` samples.

The electrical names are `Rm`, `Lm` and `Kt`: `R` and `L` are already taken
here by the wheel radius and the body length.

One lumped motor still stands for both wheels, exactly as in the existing plant.
The runtime averages the 2 encoders, commands one duty, and splits a yaw
differential after the controller; the current loop sees the mean of the 2
measured currents. `Iw` and `d` are therefore the LUMPED values.
"""
component DCPlanarDyadBot
  wheelJoint = MultibodyComponents.PlanarMechanics.OneDOFRollingWheelJoint(radius = R) {^wheelJoint}
  wheelinertia = MultibodyComponents.PlanarMechanics.Body(I = Iw, radius = 0.01, m = m, phi(initial = 0.0), w(initial = 0.0)) {^wheelinertia}
  motor = DCPlanarMotor(n_motors = n_motors, Vbus = Vbus, R = Rm, L = Lm, Kt = Kt, d = d, tau_c = tau_c, tau_s = tau_s, w_s = w_s) {^motor}
  body_mass = MultibodyComponents.PlanarMechanics.BodyShape(color = [0.2, 0.2, 0.2, 0.9], r = [0, 2 * L], r_cm = [0, L], m = M, I = Ib, radius = 0.03, body(w(initial = 0.0))) {^body_mass}
  IMU = MultibodyComponents.PlanarMechanics.AbsolutePosition(resolve_in_frame = MultibodyComponents.ResolveInFrame.World()) {^IMU}
  "PWM duty command, -1 to 1"
  duty = RealInput() {^duty}
  x = RealOutput() {^x}
  theta = RealOutput() {^theta}
  x_dot = RealOutput() {^x_dot}
  theta_dot = RealOutput() {^theta_dot}
  "Signed winding current, A"
  current = RealOutput() {^current}
  parameter M::Mass = 0.1
  parameter m::Mass = 0.05
  "Effective rolling radius, m"
  parameter R::Length = 0.04
  "Wheel axis to body centre of mass, m"
  parameter L::Length = 0.08
  parameter Ib::Inertia = 2.5e-4
  "Wheel and reflected rotor inertia of BOTH wheels, kg m^2. n_motors * tau_mech * (Kt*Kt/Rm + d)."
  parameter Iw::Inertia = 2.6220e-3
  parameter phi0::Angle = 0.0
  "How many identical motors share the one duty command. 2 on the robot."
  structural parameter n_motors::Real = 2
  "Bus voltage, V. Measured 7.68 V at full duty on 2026-09-22; a 2S pack swings 6.4 to 8.4 V over a discharge."
  parameter Vbus::Real = 7.68
  "Winding resistance of ONE motor, Ohm"
  parameter Rm::Real = 3.3864171
  "Winding inductance of ONE motor, H"
  parameter Lm::Real = 0.0185392
  "Torque and back-EMF constant of ONE motor at the wheel, N*m/A"
  parameter Kt::Real = 0.2877012
  "Viscous friction of ONE motor, N*m*s/rad"
  parameter d::Real = 7.1601e-4
  "Coulomb friction torque of ONE motor in motion, N*m"
  parameter tau_c::Real = 0.0183125
  "Static friction torque of ONE motor at rest, N*m"
  parameter tau_s::Real = 0.0447701
  "Stribeck speed, rad/s"
  parameter w_s::Real = 0.1
relations
  initial body_mass.body.phi = phi0
  connect(wheelinertia.frame_a, wheelJoint.frame_a) {^id11}
  connect(motor.frame_b, wheelJoint.frame_a) {^id12}
  connect(motor.frame_a, IMU.frame_a, body_mass.frame_a) {^id13}
  connect(duty, motor.duty) {^id14}
  connect(motor.current, current) {^id15}
  x = IMU.x
  theta = IMU.phi
  x_dot = wheelJoint.v
  theta_dot = body_mass.body.w
metadata {
  "Dyad": {
    "icons": {"default": "dyad://DyadBotComponents/PlanarDyadBot.svg"},
    "labels": [
      {
        "label": "$(instance)",
        "x": 500,
        "y": 1050,
        "rot": 0,
        "attrs": {"font-size": "120"}
      }
    ]
  },
  "_links": {
    "wheelJoint": {
      "Dyad": {
        "placement": {
          "diagram": {"iconName": "default", "x1": 340, "y1": 620, "x2": 600, "y2": 880, "rot": 0}
        },
        "tags": []
      }
    },
    "wheelinertia": {
      "Dyad": {
        "placement": {
          "diagram": {"iconName": "default", "x1": 530, "y1": 600, "x2": 670, "y2": 740, "rot": 0}
        },
        "tags": []
      }
    },
    "motor": {
      "Dyad": {
        "placement": {
          "diagram": {"iconName": "default", "x1": 420, "y1": 480, "x2": 520, "y2": 580, "rot": 90}
        },
        "tags": []
      }
    },
    "body_mass": {
      "Dyad": {
        "placement": {
